                <div class="annotation" style="margin-top:1rem;font-size:.85rem;color:var(--text-muted);line-height:1.7">
                    <strong style="color:var(--text-dim)">How this works:</strong> The house is bought in July of the start year at that year&rsquo;s median price for a new house (Census/HUD). The renter puts the buyer&rsquo;s up-front cash (down payment plus 1.04% closing costs) into bitcoin at the start year&rsquo;s average price. Every month since, through this month, the owner has paid the mortgage (if there is one), property tax (0.9% of the home&rsquo;s value a year), insurance ($2,490 a year per $400,000 of value) and maintenance (1%); the renter has paid rent and invested the difference, or sold bitcoin when rent cost more, at the price each month opened at. The house&rsquo;s value follows the S&amp;P Cotality Case-Shiller U.S. National index, which tracks the same homes over time, to its latest published month; tax, insurance and maintenance follow that value, reset each July. Rent starts at market rent for the house (Zillow&rsquo;s typical home value over its rent index, for the start year; a 2014 start uses CPI rent, since Zillow&rsquo;s index begins in 2015) and resets each July with Zillow&rsquo;s market rent index. The cost rates are today&rsquo;s defaults, applied to every year. Results are shown <em>if sold today, after tax</em> (set in Tax on a sale, above the calculator), with the before-tax figure beside each: the house less 6.6% selling costs, the loan and the tax on any gain above the home-sale exclusion; bitcoin at today&rsquo;s price less its 0.5% sale cost and the tax on its gain over what was paid, including coins sold along the way. Monthly amounts and totals are in the dollars of the day.
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="narrative"><p>The mortgage is often described as "leverage" — and it is. A 20% down payment &mdash; the conventional case &mdash; gives you 5:1 exposure to house price appreciation. But leverage has a cost. Over 30 years at typical rates, <strong>you pay roughly double the purchase price</strong> in total — the house plus the interest. Add property taxes (which never stop), insurance, and maintenance, and the all-in cost of ownership runs well above the price (The Ceiling tab adds it up). Priced in bitcoin, that asset has been deflating. <em>Holding bitcoin involves no leverage, interest, property tax or maintenance. Its costs are volatility and custody, and you still pay rent.</em></p></div>

            </div><!-- /calc-mode-retrospective -->

            <!-- PROJECTION MODE (migrated from /the-power-law.html#calculator) -->
            <div class="calc-mode-content" id="calc-mode-projection" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="projection-tab">

                <div class="section-intro"><h2>What Might Happen <em>Going Forward?</em></h2><p>The retrospective calculator above shows what <em>did</em> happen when you chose bitcoin over a house. This calculator asks the same question forward: what does the Power Law model suggest happens from here? Enter your own assumptions and compare. The projection is built on the Power Law growth model; market behavior may diverge.</p></div>

                <p class="annotation" style="font-size:.85rem;color:var(--text-muted);font-style:italic;margin:0 0 2rem;line-height:1.7">This calculator uses the <a href="/the-power-law" style="color:var(--amber)">Power Law model</a> as its bitcoin price assumption. The Power Law is an empirical observation with a 95%+ R&sup2; fit, not a guarantee. This is not investment advice.</p>

                <div class="calc-section">
                    <!-- Bitcoin scenario (rulings M3, PR 4c): one set on both pages.
                         Each moves bitcoin's multiple of the Power Law trend in a straight
                         line from today's to a target at the horizon end
                         (RealEstateModel.scenarioMultiple). Default: Stay at today's multiple. -->
                    <div style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">
                        <label style="font-size:.72rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1.5px;color:var(--text-muted);display:block;margin-bottom:.5rem">Power Law Scenario<span class="help-tip" tabindex="0">?<span class="tip-content">Each scenario moves bitcoin&rsquo;s multiple of the Power Law trend in a straight line from today&rsquo;s to a target at the horizon end. <strong>Today&rsquo;s gap persists</strong> (the default) keeps today&rsquo;s multiple: bitcoin grows at the trend&rsquo;s own rate, which slows over time, with no reversion either way. <strong>Reverts to trend.</strong> Assumes the gap to trend closes in a straight line by the horizon end. In the record, reversion has been irregular in timing. <strong>Drifts to the floor</strong> ends at 0.42&times;, the channel&rsquo;s lower bound. Since the genesis era, price has gone below it by 5.1% at most (2015); the one deep breach, 42.6%, was in 2010 (<a href="/the-bitcoin-floor" style="color:var(--amber)">see The Floor</a>). <strong>Peaks at 2.5&times; trend</strong> ends at 2.5&times;, a stress test, not a forecast: <span data-upper-record>past cycle peaks reached at least 12&times; trend (2013), 5.4&times; (2017) and 3.2&times; (2021), each lower than the last and none sustained; the peak so far in this cycle is 1.2&times; (December 2024)</span>. All four scenarios assume the Power Law trend itself continues (<a href="/the-power-law" style="color:var(--amber)">see Power Law</a>).</span></span> <a href="/the-power-law" style="color:var(--amber);text-transform:none;font-size:0.72rem;letter-spacing:0;margin-left:0.4rem;text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px dotted var(--amber-dim)">learn more &rarr;</a></label>
                        <div class="scenario-btns scenario-btns-4">
                            <button class="scenario-btn" data-scenario="floor">Drifts to the floor<br><span class="scenario-sub">ends at 0.42&times;</span></button>
                            <button class="scenario-btn active" data-scenario="stay">Today&rsquo;s gap persists<br><span class="scenario-sub">stays at <span id="fwdStayMult">today&rsquo;s</span></span></button>
                            <button class="scenario-btn" data-scenario="trend">Reverts to trend<br><span class="scenario-sub">ends at 1&times;</span></button>
                            <button class="scenario-btn" data-scenario="upper">Peaks at 2.5&times; trend<br><span class="scenario-sub">a stress test</span></button>
                        </div>
                        <p class="scenario-growth" id="fwdScenarioGrowth" aria-live="polite"></p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Purchase Method Selection -->
                    <div style="margin-bottom:1.2rem">
