                        <div class="re-chart-title">Wealth over time <span class="re-chart-frame" id="fwdChartFrame">(if sold that year, after tax)</span></div>
                        <div class="re-seg" role="group" aria-label="What the lines value"><button type="button" class="re-seg-btn active" data-basis="ifsold" aria-pressed="true">If sold, after tax</button><button type="button" class="re-seg-btn" data-basis="held" aria-pressed="false">Held</button></div>
                        <div class="re-seg" role="group" aria-label="Chart range"><button type="button" class="re-seg-btn active" data-zoom="full" aria-pressed="true">Full window</button><button type="button" class="re-seg-btn" data-zoom="first3" aria-pressed="false">First 3 years</button></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="re-chart-wrap"><canvas id="fwdChart"></canvas></div>
                    <p class="re-chart-note"><em>If sold, after tax</em> (the default) sells both in each year, as the cards value the end: the house after selling costs, the loan and any tax above the home-sale exclusion; the bitcoin after its sale cost and the tax on its gain. <em>Held</em> is before any sale: the house&rsquo;s equity and the bitcoin&rsquo;s value. The heaviest, light line is the <strong>difference</strong>, bitcoin minus the house: above zero, bitcoin is ahead. The <em>Drifts to the floor</em> scenario&rsquo;s bitcoin is drawn faintly, whichever scenario you pick.</p>
                </div>
                    <!-- The sensitivity grid (PR 7; rulings R9, P1). -->
                    <div id="fwdGrid"></div>
                    <!-- Show the calculation (PR 6a; rulings P2, P7). -->
                    <div id="fwdLedger"></div>
                </div>

                <div class="annotation" style="margin-top:1rem;font-size:.85rem;color:var(--text-muted);line-height:1.7">
                    <strong style="color:var(--text-dim)">How this works:</strong> The bitcoin projection uses the <a href="/the-power-law" style="color:var(--amber)">Power Law model</a> (Porkopolis coefficients). Home appreciation is nominal, like the mortgage rate: the default is the recorded US rate since 2000 (Case-Shiller National, 4.68% a year to 2025), with since-1990 and long-run presets in Baseline assumptions. Mortgage assumes your chosen down payment (default 20%), 30-year fixed. Both paths run the same number of years from today. <strong style="color:var(--text-dim)">Equal cash out:</strong> the renter puts the buyer&rsquo;s up-front cash (down payment plus closing costs) into bitcoin, then each month pays rent and invests the owner&rsquo;s cost minus rent, or sells bitcoin when rent costs more; the owner&rsquo;s cost is the mortgage payment plus property tax (0.9%), insurance and maintenance (1%), the last three following the home&rsquo;s value. Rent defaults to market rent for the price (Zillow&rsquo;s price-to-rent ratio, 15.77) and grows with home prices. Bitcoin&rsquo;s price moves month by month from today&rsquo;s multiple of the Power Law trend to the scenario&rsquo;s target at the horizon; the default, Today&rsquo;s gap persists, keeps today&rsquo;s multiple, so no reversion to trend is assumed. Each purchase and sale pays 0.5%. Results are shown <em>if sold</em>, after tax (set in Tax on a sale, above the calculator), with the before-tax figure beside each: the house less 6.6% selling costs, the loan and the tax on any gain above the home-sale exclusion; bitcoin less its 0.5% sale cost and the tax on its gain over what was paid, including coins sold along the way. Every default is in Baseline assumptions, with its source. <em>Projected dollar amounts render in your chosen frame &mdash; Real (today&rsquo;s purchasing power) or Nominal (future dollars) &mdash; via the toggle above the results. Every end value is divided by the same inflation factor in the Real view, so it never changes which path is ahead. Monthly amounts and totals are shown nominal, as paid.</em>
                </div>

            </div><!-- /calc-mode-projection -->

